$ jailops app deny db from @web --dry-run
Dry run: nothing was changed. Denying @web on db would do:
  - firewall policy app-db: remove allow in tcp from 10.20.0.11 to port 5432
  SQL: ALTER ROLE "web" NOLOGIN PASSWORD NULL;
Note: role web keeps its database and loses its login and password; --drop-role drops it

$ jailops app deny db from @web
Denied @web on db.
  - firewall policy app-db: remove allow in tcp from 10.20.0.11 to port 5432
  SQL: ALTER ROLE "web" NOLOGIN PASSWORD NULL;
Note: role web keeps its database and loses its login and password; --drop-role drops it

$ jailops app deny db from @web
Note: nothing changed: @web has no access to db

$ jailops app deny db from 192.0.2.0/24 --drop-role -y
Denied 192.0.2.0/24 on db.
  - firewall policy app-db: remove allow in tcp from 192.0.2.0/24 to port 5432
  SQL: REASSIGN OWNED BY "shop" TO postgres;
  SQL: DROP OWNED BY "shop";
  SQL: DROP ROLE IF EXISTS "shop";
Note: role shop is dropped; what it owned now belongs to postgres and database shop is kept

$ jailops app allow db from 192.0.2.0/24
! invalid: name the database of this grant with --db NAME (a source that is not a jail has no default)
! hint: Read the message, correct the named field and retry; 'jailops firewall validate' checks rules offline. (jailops explain invalid)
[exit 2]

